#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186105525 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186105738 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "02d46a44-0f02-5491-8e71-2eb22ad50ff9" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating the Measure of an Angle Using the Isosceles Triangle Theorem"}] :question/id #uuid "df6c46c7-a65c-578c-a9b3-e75de73c527a" :question/math-academy-id "e-2718" :question/problem "What is the measure of $\\angle{A}$ in the triangle $\\triangle{ABC}$ below?\n\n![](images/91/91d1c211d33bb42a474242a6895330a3a2b77b50450d8ba7a1b533cb0a45f3b9.png)" :question/worked-solution "Since two sides are congruent, $\\overline{BC} \\cong \\overline{AB},$ we conclude that $\\triangle ABC$ is isosceles. Consequently, the base angles must have the same measure:\n\n$$\nm\\angle C = m\\angle A = x -10^\\circ\n$$\n\nNow, since the internal angles of any triangle sum to $180^\\circ,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}m∠A+m∠B+m∠C & ={180}^{∘} \\\\ (x-{10}^{∘})+(x+{20}^{∘})+(x-{10}^{∘}) & ={180}^{∘} \\\\ 3x & ={180}^{∘} \\\\ x & ={60}^{∘}\\,.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, substituting $x=60^\\circ$ into the equation for the measure of $\\angle A$ gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}m∠A & =x-{10}^{∘} \\\\  & ={60}^{∘}-{10}^{∘} \\\\  & ={50}^{∘}\\,.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo $m\\angle A = 50^\\circ.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186105526 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186105739 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "0690d62b-432c-5d1e-96f2-b43393f6efa9" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating the Measure of an Angle in an Isosceles Triangle Given an Exterior Angle"}] :question/id #uuid "c7ae19b8-aee0-5a64-b7c8-61f3ce75272c" :question/math-academy-id "e-2721" :question/problem "What is the measure of $\\angle{H}$ in the triangle $\\triangle{FGH}$ below?\n\n![](images/b1/b13dd7712b6ed41bbd6ba9db85b489f71fb498c711d5dd67d4dd91a0845b5dcd.png)" :question/worked-solution "First, from the diagram, notice that $\\angle{HFG}$ and $\\angle{EFH}$ are supplementary. Therefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}m∠HFG+m∠EFH & ={180}^{∘} \\\\ m∠HFG+{105}^{∘} & ={180}^{∘} \\\\ m∠HFG & ={75}^{∘}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince $\\triangle{FGH}$ is an isosceles triangle where $\\overline{GH} \\cong \\overline{FH}$, the base angles must be equal:\n\n$$\nm\\angle{G} = m\\angle{HFG}=75^\\circ.\n$$\n\nFinally, since the interior angles of any triangle sum to $180^\\circ,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}m∠G+m∠HFG+m∠H & ={180}^{∘} \\\\ {75}^{∘}+{75}^{∘}+m∠H & ={180}^{∘} \\\\ {150}^{∘}+m∠H & ={180}^{∘} \\\\ m∠H & ={30}^{∘}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186105527 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186105741 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "0044ba61-80c8-57cf-80dc-8a17cf980a84" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Measure of an Angle Given Multiple Triangles"}] :question/id #uuid "e44e7e25-f65b-5f40-9aef-cbe55bfd1b53" :question/math-academy-id "e-2726" :question/problem "In the figure below, $m\\angle{CAD}=100^\\circ.$ What is the measure of $\\angle{D}?$\n\n![](images/59/59bb54f508b8045eeffb7cdbf547b6be0f074009b3288fb13e115159909782a8.png)" :question/worked-solution "First, notice that $\\triangle{ABC}$ is an equilateral triangle. Consequently, all of the interior angles must be equal to $60^\\circ\\mathbin{:}$ \n\n$$\nm\\angle{BAC}=m\\angle{ABC}=m\\angle{C}=60^\\circ\n$$\n\nAlso, since $\\triangle{ADB}$ is an isosceles triangle, the base angles must have equal measure:\n\n$$\nm\\angle{BAD}=m\\angle{ABD}\n$$\n\n![](images/a9/a997837f549d59b2a554bf6bc60d9de3ebf6b4b97188ac3d0f07342fb4af6507.png)\n\nNow, since we're told that $m\\angle{CAD}=100^\\circ,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}m∠BAD & =m∠CAD-m∠BAC \\\\  & ={100}^{∘}-{60}^{∘} \\\\  & ={40}^{∘}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, in $\\triangle{BAD}$, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}m∠BAD+m∠D+m∠ABD & ={180}^{∘} \\\\ 2m∠BAD+m∠D & ={180}^{∘} \\\\ 2\\cdot {40}^{∘}+m∠D & ={180}^{∘} \\\\ {80}^{∘}+m∠D & ={180}^{∘} \\\\ m∠D & ={100}^{∘}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186105584 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186105740 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "c9c2b7ee-d32b-550b-b110-81d04d750c98" :knowledge-point/title "Solving for a Variable Given Unknown Lengths in an Isosceles Triangle"}] :question/id #uuid "d9e953e1-5af0-55eb-a861-af6f4ed9b720" :question/math-academy-id "e-4269" :question/problem "![](images/84/842cd509d5d998858936dda5ae07cc17c69cfd83ea86881ba542d8d34bff7cc6.png)\n\nWhat is the measure of $\\overline{AB}$ in the isosceles triangle $ABC$ above?" :question/worked-solution "Since $\\triangle ABC$ is an isosceles triangle with base $\\overline{AB}$, we have that $\\overline{BC} \\cong \\overline{AC}.$ Hence,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}BC & =AC \\\\ 4x-3 & =2x+1 \\\\ 2x & =4 \\\\ x & =2\\,.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting this value into the expression for the measure of $\\overline{AB}$ gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}AB & =x+4 \\\\  & =(2)+4 \\\\  & =6\\,.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe conclude that $AB=6.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186505752 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186105738 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "02d46a44-0f02-5491-8e71-2eb22ad50ff9" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating the Measure of an Angle Using the Isosceles Triangle Theorem"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "02691191-a222-5e50-b003-388e2c6df4a6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{87}^{∘}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186505108} {:answer/id #uuid "1a37cf3c-005f-5a4b-aeac-abaf78c3a7d7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{83}^{∘}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186505150} {:answer/id #uuid "5d9e6a70-c971-5bac-8559-77499b986da7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{85}^{∘}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186505295} {:answer/id #uuid "9b7ccc92-79c7-5431-a394-1f8287934666" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{89}^{∘}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186505410} {:answer/id #uuid "dc9bb2da-7645-5f43-9a6e-24089049974f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{86}^{∘}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186505532}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "dc9bb2da-7645-5f43-9a6e-24089049974f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{86}^{∘}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186505532} :answer-field/id #uuid "ffd31e8b-b3ec-560a-b1c8-c443d4f6582d" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186505726}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "2f01ee13-fe5d-5b17-b506-bfc6a72b2888" :question/math-academy-id "q-118661" :question/problem "![](images/4d/4da332c74f255825ae0073ca41117ae477f72262ef8d45911b9ce48ed3b8ccf0.png)\n\nWhat is the measure of $∠G$ in the triangle $△FGH$ above?" :question/worked-solution "Since two sides are congruent, $\\overset{―}{FG}≅\\overset{―}{GH},$ we conclude that $△FGH$ is isosceles. Consequently, the base angles must have the same measure:\n\n$$\nm∠F=m∠H=x+{13}^{∘}\n$$\n\nNow, since the internal angles of any triangle sum to ${180}^{∘},$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}m∠F+m∠G+m∠H & ={180}^{∘} \\\\ (x+{13}^{∘})+(2x+{18}^{∘})+(x+{13}^{∘}) & ={180}^{∘} \\\\ 4x+{44}^{∘} & ={180}^{∘} \\\\ 4x & ={136}^{∘} \\\\ x & ={34}^{∘}\\,.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, substituting $x={34}^{∘}$ into the equation for the measure of $∠G$ gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}m∠G & =2x+{18}^{∘} \\\\  & =2({34}^{∘})+{18}^{∘} \\\\  & ={86}^{∘}\\,.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo $m∠G={86}^{∘}.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186505815 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186105739 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "0690d62b-432c-5d1e-96f2-b43393f6efa9" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating the Measure of an Angle in an Isosceles Triangle Given an Exterior Angle"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "a1723209-b261-53ec-9c92-067b43c991cd" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{78}^{∘}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186505424} {:answer/id #uuid "b06b543d-07f0-55f8-b6aa-b4c57d998ca9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{81}^{∘}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186505451} {:answer/id #uuid "bf1508d2-3625-57e0-8cdc-fb91f4d4dd82" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{77}^{∘}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186505475} {:answer/id #uuid "d468839f-1657-5c4f-b5b6-5799add32068" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{75}^{∘}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186505516} {:answer/id #uuid "dfff6e7d-f412-5719-8768-9faaec5eed7b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{80}^{∘}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186505539}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "a1723209-b261-53ec-9c92-067b43c991cd" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{78}^{∘}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186505424} :answer-field/id #uuid "afe57a34-7063-5007-9fab-e9e8d3248327" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186505690}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "c42a06c6-86aa-5826-a78d-ab4a4cc2e08a" :question/math-academy-id "q-118675" :question/problem "![](images/e4/e4600221042deaad471de628179200fa62872fdfe38220ca66eaded966f8894e.png)\n\nFind the value of $w$ in the triangle shown above." :question/worked-solution "First, from the diagram, notice that $∠RTS$ and $∠STU$ are supplementary. Therefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}m∠RTS+m∠STU & ={180}^{∘} \\\\ m∠RTS+w & ={180}^{∘} \\\\ m∠RTS & ={180}^{∘}-w.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince $△RST$ is an isosceles triangle where $\\overset{―}{RT}≅\\overset{―}{TS}$, the base angles must be equal:\n\n$$\nm∠S=m∠R={39}^{∘}.\n$$\n\nFinally, since the interior angles of any triangle sum to ${180}^{∘},$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}m∠R+m∠S+m∠RTS & ={180}^{∘} \\\\ {39}^{∘}+{39}^{∘}+({180}^{∘}-w) & ={180}^{∘} \\\\ {39}^{∘}+{39}^{∘}-w & =0 \\\\ w & ={78}^{∘}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186505818 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186105740 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "c9c2b7ee-d32b-550b-b110-81d04d750c98" :knowledge-point/title "Solving for a Variable Given Unknown Lengths in an Isosceles Triangle"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "3a3430dd-2a88-5cfd-94e2-4ef72aa0f912" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "111" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186505207} {:answer/id #uuid "3c0a90fc-dbab-59c4-90bb-f10642d66abe" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "88" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186505214} {:answer/id #uuid "4ea46e62-57fa-5e6a-8a1d-b3bc4a07e653" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "121" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186505261} {:answer/id #uuid "5f248e42-272d-5cb8-ad97-86dcebabdb4d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "110" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186505297} {:answer/id #uuid "bb559f99-484b-51d7-968a-ce70bc8be11e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "134" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186505468}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "4ea46e62-57fa-5e6a-8a1d-b3bc4a07e653" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "121" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186505261} :answer-field/id #uuid "3723024c-cd1f-50ee-aa66-c7cfc430c8a4" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186505636}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "cfe6f4b2-4744-5b79-aa1c-bad69130f89a" :question/math-academy-id "q-118817" :question/problem "![](images/87/876a5edd5e10017ffb5f858481294d121b0cfec87e936538ffde0ceb9af5c663.png)\n\nThe triangle $△TUV$ shown above is isosceles with the base $\\overset{―}{TV}.$ What is the perimeter of $△TUV?$" :question/worked-solution "Since $△TUV$ is an isosceles triangle with base $\\overset{―}{TV}$, we have that $\\overset{―}{TU}≅\\overset{―}{UV}.$ Hence,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}TU & =UV \\\\ 5x-8 & =4x+3 \\\\ x & =11.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe perimeter of $△TUV$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}p & =TU+UV+TV \\\\  & =(5x-8)+(4x+3)+(2x+5) \\\\  & =11x.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, substituting $x=11$ in the expression for the perimeter, we get\n\n$$\np=11x=11(11)=121.\n$$\n\nHence, $p=121.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186505837 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186105741 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "0044ba61-80c8-57cf-80dc-8a17cf980a84" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Measure of an Angle Given Multiple Triangles"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "8c0abbb6-f8b6-5629-8bf7-efbca658aa53" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{67}^{∘}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186505377} {:answer/id #uuid "947119b6-be54-5bf8-8fea-6fdf1288eb43" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{55}^{∘}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186505389} {:answer/id #uuid "94ca3912-e9df-5aad-9f35-f1dfc7eb6279" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{65}^{∘}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186505391} {:answer/id #uuid "ccb7d431-9e21-5085-822a-5db344f8a02a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{60}^{∘}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186505500} {:answer/id #uuid "ed99da56-c6d1-5ad1-a054-b48dc6be15dd" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{63}^{∘}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186505565}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "94ca3912-e9df-5aad-9f35-f1dfc7eb6279" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{65}^{∘}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186505391} :answer-field/id #uuid "76b96617-6d7f-5cdb-9d08-1c3ef885522b" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186505659}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "ee71d401-4421-55e1-931e-7e917f7688f0" :question/math-academy-id "q-118950" :question/problem "![](images/dc/dcd036443bb904abafd7ad0cc2aea6ead68538835c08d8b99958291f393a4476.png)\n\nWhat is the measure of $∠S$ above?" :question/worked-solution "First, notice that $△PQR$ is an equilateral triangle. Consequently, all of the interior angles must be equal to ${60}^{∘}:$ \n\n$$\nm∠P=m∠PQR=m∠QRP={60}^{∘}\n$$\n\nAlso, since $△QRS$ is an isosceles triangle, the base angles must have equal measure:\n\n$$\nm∠RQS=m∠S\n$$\n\n![](images/79/792155c685456f6c278fff5a0341f67358a8b0983d696cf55a6867c9c71ec7cb.png)\n\nNotice also that $∠QRP$, $∠SRQ$ and $∠TRS$ are supplementary, therefore, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}m∠QRP+m∠SRQ+m∠TRS & ={180}^{∘} \\\\ {60}^{∘}+m∠SRQ+{70}^{∘} & ={180}^{∘} \\\\ m∠SRQ+{130}^{∘} & ={180}^{∘} \\\\ m∠SRQ & ={50}^{∘}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, in $△QRS$, using the fact that the interior angles of a triangle sum to ${180}^{∘}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}m∠RQS+m∠S+m∠SRQ & ={180}^{∘} \\\\ m∠S+m∠S+{50}^{∘} & ={180}^{∘} \\\\ 2m∠S & ={130}^{∘} \\\\ m∠S & ={65}^{∘}\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186505731 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186105739 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "0690d62b-432c-5d1e-96f2-b43393f6efa9" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating the Measure of an Angle in an Isosceles Triangle Given an Exterior Angle"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "06b297e5-0321-5cb8-ba70-a7357cf14e13" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "112" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186505118}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "06b297e5-0321-5cb8-ba70-a7357cf14e13" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "112" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186505118} :answer-field/id #uuid "bca22090-6107-5000-a622-c53df9beee48" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186505699}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "02e57395-d812-5a19-a50a-4f3093174d3b" :question/math-academy-id "q-171893" :question/problem "![](images/fb/fb0478d757190db93eeccddda17f122be97230308655eeb0983add2a316866a5.png)\n\nIn the triangle above, the value of $y$ is{{field-1}}${}^{∘}.$" :question/worked-solution "First, from the diagram, notice that $∠BCA$ and $∠BCD$ are supplementary. Therefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}m∠BCA+m∠BCD & ={180}^{∘} \\\\ m∠BCA+y & ={180}^{∘} \\\\ m∠BCA & ={180}^{∘}-y.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince $△ABC$ is an isosceles triangle where $\\overset{―}{AC}≅\\overset{―}{BC}$, the base angles must be equal:\n\n$$\nm∠B=m∠A={56}^{∘}.\n$$\n\nFinally, since the interior angles of any triangle sum to ${180}^{∘},$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}m∠A+m∠B+m∠BCA & ={180}^{∘} \\\\ {56}^{∘}+{56}^{∘}+({180}^{∘}-y) & ={180}^{∘} \\\\ y & ={112}^{∘}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186505742 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186105741 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "0044ba61-80c8-57cf-80dc-8a17cf980a84" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Measure of an Angle Given Multiple Triangles"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "ef6fa6a3-2eab-5526-b415-5da01b5cf861" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "36" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186505569}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "ef6fa6a3-2eab-5526-b415-5da01b5cf861" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "36" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186505569} :answer-field/id #uuid "b91626e3-0a70-5ba0-81e2-883eb4b5223c" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186505697}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "1cbf3ae4-b692-5171-8065-1b037cf779e6" :question/math-academy-id "q-171896" :question/problem "![](images/bb/bbc303a7d1ee4bcad3bdbd29b4897e972556bfe89bdff2831aaeef642026b98b.png)\n\nIn the diagram above, the measure of $∠D$ is equal to{{field-1}}${}^{∘}.$" :question/worked-solution "First, notice that $△ABC$ is an isosceles triangle. Consequently, the base angles must have equal measure:\n\n$$\nm∠A=m∠ABC={72}^{∘}\n$$\n\nAlso, since $△CDB$ is an isosceles triangle, we have:\n\n$$\nm∠D=m∠BCD\n$$\n\nSince $∠ABC$ and $∠CBD$ are supplementary, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}m∠ABC+m∠CBD & ={180}^{∘} \\\\ {72}^{∘}+m∠CBD & ={180}^{∘} \\\\ m∠CBD & ={108}^{∘}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we calculate $m∠D$ using the fact that the interior angles of a triangle sum to ${180}^{∘}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}m∠D+m∠BCD+m∠CBD & ={180}^{∘} \\\\ m∠D+m∠D+{108}^{∘} & ={180}^{∘} \\\\ 2m∠D & ={72}^{∘} \\\\ m∠D & ={36}^{∘}\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186505797 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186105740 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "c9c2b7ee-d32b-550b-b110-81d04d750c98" :knowledge-point/title "Solving for a Variable Given Unknown Lengths in an Isosceles Triangle"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "33789998-29f6-5249-a338-28872495f28c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "5" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186505195} {:answer/id #uuid "34d7776e-9158-50f7-8089-722db7df5376" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186505199} {:answer/id #uuid "76d9d46a-138d-5b6e-9d55-b7e14c6c27de" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "7" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186505346} {:answer/id #uuid "c409a004-37c0-5a78-9094-3b282436335a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "8" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186505485} {:answer/id #uuid "d3ab559c-b189-5f62-96a0-04db72b15a12" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "6" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186505514}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "d3ab559c-b189-5f62-96a0-04db72b15a12" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "6" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186505514} :answer-field/id #uuid "0df4cd81-2779-55e2-9b8a-f7cdb56d9688" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186505611}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "9d3dcdfc-6059-5bce-b1ec-1f29a3baad3b" :question/math-academy-id "q-68286" :question/problem "![](images/c1/c182b73af6ff37c3c673fe0e174e8f86e2f89a0d38ed385f64e8b8b3a4731fc8.png)\n\nThe triangle $△ABC$ shown above is isosceles with base $\\overset{―}{BC}.$ What is the measure of $\\overset{―}{BC}?$" :question/worked-solution "Since $△ABC$ is an isosceles triangle with base $\\overset{―}{BC}$, we have that $\\overset{―}{AB}≅\\overset{―}{AC}.$ Hence,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}AB & =AC \\\\ 3x-3 & =2x+2 \\\\ x & =5\\,.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting this value into the expression for the measure of $\\overset{―}{BC}$ gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}BC & =2x-4 \\\\  & =2(5)-4 \\\\  & =6\\,.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186505835 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186105738 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "02d46a44-0f02-5491-8e71-2eb22ad50ff9" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating the Measure of an Angle Using the Isosceles Triangle Theorem"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "1ab71926-9e86-5df1-a1bb-fd736c9137be" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{37}^{∘}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186505151} {:answer/id #uuid "3c9a5fcf-b34a-548a-b949-345148f543b6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{35}^{∘}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186505217} {:answer/id #uuid "569b9ce0-1473-55bc-a6de-af288bdec9b1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{34}^{∘}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186505278} {:answer/id #uuid "6decc0f1-591f-5edf-b443-7609212d6497" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{39}^{∘}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186505326} {:answer/id #uuid "808b0c6e-d096-5504-9924-734f0194e469" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{40}^{∘}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186505361}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "1ab71926-9e86-5df1-a1bb-fd736c9137be" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{37}^{∘}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186505151} :answer-field/id #uuid "0fb07700-e768-5a8e-8424-3dc71e068bd6" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186505612}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "ebdd2a76-ec62-5920-99df-a78d7b00016a" :question/math-academy-id "q-74856" :question/problem "![](images/db/dbc4368e30cfd0543d085fc8baa644031b35f4086f2da53d5aaecf6b4b626245.png)\n\nWhat is the measure of $∠B$ in the triangle  $△ABC$ above?" :question/worked-solution "Since two sides are congruent, $\\overset{―}{AC}≅\\overset{―}{CB},$ we conclude that $△ABC$ is isosceles. Consequently, the base angles must have the same measure:\n\n$$\nm∠A=m∠B=2x-{9}^{∘}\n$$\n\nNow, since the internal angles of any triangle sum to ${180}^{∘},$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}m∠A+m∠B+m∠C & ={180}^{∘} \\\\ (2x-{9}^{∘})+(2x-{9}^{∘})+(4x+{14}^{∘}) & ={180}^{∘} \\\\ 8x-{4}^{∘} & ={180}^{∘} \\\\ 8x & ={184}^{∘} \\\\ x & ={23}^{∘}\\,.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, substituting $x={23}^{∘}$ into the equation for the measure of $∠B$ gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}m∠B & =m∠A \\\\  & =2x-{9}^{∘} \\\\  & =2({23}^{∘})-{9}^{∘} \\\\  & ={37}^{∘}\\,.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo $m∠B={37}^{∘}.$"}]}
